12. cite33†B Details of Models L44:   13. cite34†C Evaluation Results L45:   14. cite35†D Explanation for Figure  L46:   15. cite36†E Difference from Instruction Following L47:   16. cite37†F Construction Steps L48:     1. cite38†Audio filtering. L49:     2. cite39†Quality Validation Annotators. L50:   17. cite40†G Details of Evaluation Metrics L51:     1. cite41†Categorical decisions L52:     2. cite42†Graded qualities L53:     3. cite43†Why these metrics are chosen? L54:     4. cite44†Modality Reliance. L55:     5. cite45†Coherence. L56:     6. cite46†Discrimination. L57:     7. cite47†Naturalness. L58:     8. cite48†Supportiveness. L59:     9. cite49†Delivery and Emotion L60:   18. cite50†H Evaluation Metric Scores L61:     1. cite51†H.1 Linguistic Features L62:       1. cite52†Q1. Modality Reliance (M.R.) L63:       2. cite53†Q2. Naturalness (Nat.) L64:       3. cite54†Q3. Coherence (Coh.) L65:       4. cite55†Q4. Supportiveness (Sup.) L66:       5. cite56†Q5. Discrimination (Disc.) L67:     2. cite57†H.2 Paralinguistic Features (Audio Assessment) L68:       1. cite58†Q6. Delivery L69:   19. cite59†I Human Annotation for Responses L70:   20. cite60†J Fine-grained Paralinguistic Evaluation L71: cite61†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L72: \nL73: arXiv:2601.10513v1 [cs.CL] 15 Jan 2026\nL74: # AEQ-Bench: Measuring Empathy of Omni-Modal Large Models\nL75: Xuan Luo Affiliation: The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong Affiliation: The Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen    Lewei Yao Affiliation: Huawei, Hong Kong    Libo Zhao Affiliation: The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong    Lanqing Hong Affiliation: Huawei, Hong Kong    Kai Chen Affiliation: Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Hong Kong    Dehua Tao Affiliation: Huawei, Hong Kong    Daxin Tan Affiliation: Huawei, Hong Kong    Ruifeng Xu Affiliation: The Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen Affiliation: Shenzhen Loop Area Institute, Shenzhen Correspondence: jing-amelia.li@polyu.edu.hk    Jing Li Affiliation: The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong\nL76: ###### Abstract\nL77: While the automatic evaluation of omni-modal large models (OLMs) is essential, assessing empathy remains a significant challenge due to its inherent affectivity.\nL78: To investigate this challenge, we introduce AEQ-Bench (Audio Empathy Quotient Benchmark), a novel benchmark to systematically assess two core empathetic capabilities of OLMs: (i) generating empathetic responses by comprehending affective cues from multi-modal inputs (audio + text), and (ii) judging the empathy of audio responses without relying on text transcription. Compared to existing benchmarks, AEQ-Bench incorporates two novel settings that vary in context specificity and speech tone.\nL79: Comprehensive assessment across linguistic and paralinguistic metrics reveals that (1) OLMs trained with audio output capabilities generally outperformed models with text-only outputs, and (2) while OLMs align with human judgments for coarse-grained quality assessment, they remain unreliable for evaluating fine-grained paralinguistic expressiveness.\nL80: ## 1 Introduction\nL81: Empathy is the ability to understand, share, and respond to the feelings and experiences of another person by taking their perspective cite62†Szalita (1976) —essentially, to “put oneself in another’s shoes”. It is crucial for NLP models to gain such capabilities for providing positive user experiences during interactions with humans.\nL82: This need is becoming more pressing yet challenging as omni-modal large models (OLMs) become the popular backbone, which integrate modalities such as audio, vision, and text to enable more human-like interactions (cite63†Wu et al., 2023 ; cite64†Yin et al., 2024 ).\nL83: While OLMs grow in complexity, the automatic evaluation of empathy becomes crucial for training paradigms like reinforcement learning. However, this task is non-trivial, as empathy is inherently affective. Consequently, establishing a benchmark to quantify nuanced emotional resonance across audio and textual modalities remains a significant open challenge.\nL84: Most existing benchmarks focus on cognitive abilities, such as knowledge retrieval, complex reasoning, and instruction following (cite65†Yue et al., 2024 ; cite66†Zhang et al., 2025c ) (see Fig. cite67†1 ), largely overlooking empathy evaluation. As OLMs become increasingly human-like, we aim to benchmark their capacity to generate empathetic responses and judge empathy in diverse contexts.\nL85: Human communication conveys empathy through not only the linguistic content of what is said but also the paralinguistic tone of how it is said. For instance, the utterance “It was a curious coincidence” implies genuine surprise or delight when delivered with warmth. However, a cool, sarcastic delivery of the same words suggests scepticism or criticism. To be truly effective, an OLM should understand and generate both the right words and the appropriate acoustic cues.\nL86: cite68†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Overview of omni-modal tasks: instruction following (a & b) and conversation (c & d). The input configurations are: (a) audio content with textual instruction; (b) audio instruction with text, audio, or image content; (c) unimodal dialogue; and (d) mixed-modality dialogue (demonstrated by AEQ-Bench on the Meld subset).\nL87: In the mixed-modality setting, the text provides context (e.g., chat history summary) for the current audio utterance, requiring the model to respond accordingly. Outputs across all tasks may be text, audio, or both.\nL88: Previous NLP research has prioritised linguistic features (e.g., lexical choice and explicit emotional statements) for empathy, while paralinguistic cues (e.g., prosody and acoustic delivery) are equally critical for audio-capable OLMs cite69†Aziz-Zadeh et al. (2010) . However, a significant research gap persists in their joint examination. Recent studies reduced audio to mere transcriptions cite70†Wang et al. (2025) in order to rely on established text-based metrics cite71†Zhang et al.\nL89: (2025a) , ignoring the nuances of vocal delivery. This limitation presents a fundamental question: Can OLMs automatically deliver and judge empathy by accounting for both linguistic and paralinguistic cues like human?\nL90: To answer this question, we introduce AEQ-Bench, a benchmark designed to assess the empathic responsiveness and judgment capabilities of OLMs. It comprises 1,885 English instances, each pairing an audio utterance with a concise textual context. It overcomes the limitations of existing empathetic dialogue evaluations, which lack multimodality (see Fig. cite67†1 ) and parallel comparisons (over varying contexts and acoustic tones).\nL91: There exhibits two novel design strategies: (i) Contextual Variation, where distinct background contexts are applied to the same utterance to alter its pragmatic meaning (Fig. cite72†2 ); and (ii) Tonal Variation, where identical contexts are paired with the same utterance delivered in varying emotional tones to shift user intent (Fig. cite73†3 ).\nL92: To the best of our knowledge, AEQ-Bench is the first benchmark to jointly examine linguistic and paralinguistic empathy via parallel contexts and acoustic tonal variations.\nL93: We then conducted an extensive evaluation on AEQ-Bench, benchmarking current state-of-the-art OLMs on their ability to generate empathetic responses and judge generations from other models compared to human evaluators. This assessment covered seven dimensions designed to comprehensively measure both linguistic and paralinguistic features: modality reliance, naturalness, coherence, supportiveness, discrimination, and delivery.\nL94: Experimental results demonstrate that OLMs trained with audio output capabilities generally outperform those limited to text-only output. GPT produced the most human-like and empathetic narratives, followed closely by Qwen-Omni. These findings underscore the value of integrating text and audio for learning empathy. However, while OLMs align with human judgment on coarse-grained tasks, they remain unreliable for fine-grained emotional evaluation.\nL95: Specifically, the models lack paralinguistic empathy, often delivering flat speech, and OLM-based judges diverge significantly from human perception regarding emotional prosody. Consequently, generating and evaluating expressive empathetic tone remains an open challenge.\nL96: cite74†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: The constructed GigaSpeech subset of AEQ-Bench featuring context variation. For each utterance, we construct two plausible contexts, each associated with a corresponding reference response. (Appx. cite35†D ) cite75†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: The constructed Emovdb subset of AEQ-Bench with tone variation. The middle part is the constructed context and the original audio. The utterances with different emotions/tones share the same context.\nL97: Each audio tone (amused, disgusted, angry, and neutral) has a plausible explanation for its context and a corresponding response.\nL98: ## 2 Related Work\nL99: #### Empathy and Emotional Intelligence.\nL100: Empathy is widely defined as the ability to perceive, understand, and respond to another individual’s emotional states. It is fundamental for satisfactory and effective conversational communication cite62†Szalita (1976) . Empathy in dialogue has been studied in text through datasets and frameworks that annotate empathic responses and strategies. EmpatheticDialogues introduced 25k conversations grounded in emotional situations for empathic response generation cite76†Rashkin et al. (2019) .\nL101: Work in mental-health support provides theory-grounded taxonomies and labelled corpora to detect and analyse empathy mechanisms in text cite77†Sharma et al. (2020) . With the development of LLMs, automatic evaluation of response empathy is available cite71†Zhang et al. (2025a) . For generated audio responses, it remains confined to the text modality empathy evaluation, converting the audio content into text cite70†Wang et al. (2025) .\nL102: In contrast, we evaluate the potential of OLMs as automatic judges directly on the audio response, in order to study empathy from both linguistic and paralinguistic perspectives.\nL103: Subset  | Source  | Language  | Core Contribution  | Length\nL104: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL105: Meld  | TV Series  | Colloquial  | Daily Conversational Emotion  | 18, 30\nL106: EmoV-DB  | Studio recording  | Literary  | Explicit Emotional Range  | 10, 20\nL107: GigaSpeech  | Streaming content  | Formal  | Domain Diversity, Acoustic Variability  | 22, 17\nL108: Table 1: Contribution of each data source to AEQ-Bench. EmoV-DB and Meld primarily contain audios with clear emotions. Length is the average words of the utterances and generated contexts in AEQ-Bench.\nL109: #### Paralinguistic Research and Audio Cues.\nL110: Human language is generally split into the verbal channel (the semantic content or chosen words) and the vocal channel (the non-semantic delivery  cite78†(1958) . Paralinguistic features belong to this vocal channel, conveying emotional meaning through prosody, which is variations in pitch, loudness, timbre, speech rate, and pauses. These paralinguistic cues are indispensable for empathetic communication cite79†Loveys et al.\nL111: (2021) because they can drastically alter the meaning of verbal content, such as in the case of sarcasm, or reveal emotional states that text transcription alone cannot capture. MFCCs (Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients) cite80†Davis and Mermelstein (1990) , for instance, capture the characteristics of the sound that are most discernible to the human ear. Meld cite81†Poria et al. (2019) and IEMOCAP cite82†Busso et al. (2008) are widely used benchmark for SER. EmoV-DB cite83†Adigwe et al.\nL112: (2018) is an emotional speech dataset used for synthesis and generation purposes, offering varied content in different tones delivered by both male and female voices. Furthermore, specialised datasets, such as VocalSound cite84†Gong et al. (2022) , provide collections of non-lexical human vocalisations, including laughter and sighs, to train and evaluate models across a broader range of human-voice acoustics.\nL113: For multimodal dialogue, the evaluation on generated audio quality is conducted by human listeners rating the naturalness or quality of the speech cite85†Zhang et al. (2025b) ; cite86†Xu et al. (2025a) ; cite87†Du et al. (2024) , using Mean Opinion Score (MOS) cite88†Viswanathan and Viswanathan (2005) . However, OLMs’ sensitivity to paralinguistic perception, such as tone and emotions, remains underexplored.\nL114: AEQ-Bench fills the gaps by setting the dialogue with both text and human audio, thereby evaluating the multi-modal empathetic conversation ability and investigating the feasibility of automatic speech evaluation using OLMs.\nL115: ## 3 AEQ-Bench Benchmark\nL116: ### 3.1 Benchmark Design\nL117: Empathy broadly refers to our reaction to the experiences of others. It has two major components: 1) Cognitive empathy, defined as the process of understanding another person’s perspective^{1}^{1} 1 In real human interaction, most daily conversations are informational or neutral. In these settings, empathy is expressed not through emotional mirroring but through helpfulness, perspective-taking, alignment with the interlocutor’s goals, and context-appropriate support.\nL118: and 2) Affective empathy, also called emotional empathy, defined as an observer’s emotional response to the affective state of others cite89†Davis (1983) .\nL119: AEQ-Bench for evaluating OLMs’ empathy is constructed from three complementary datasets: Meld cite81†Poria et al. (2019) , GigaSpeech cite90†Chen et al. (2021) , and EmoV-DB cite83†Adigwe et al. (2018) . They span the critical dimensions of real-world communication: contextual dialogue, domain diversity, and explicit emotional expression.^{2}^{2} 2 We exclusively employ human speech to ensure natural paralinguistic variability (e.g., accents, prosody), a complexity absent in stable synthesised voices.\nL120: The contexts are constructed in reverse according to the Utterance. The features of each data source are present in Table cite91†1 and the composition is listed in Table cite92†2 . It is classified into two types (985 chatting + 900 asking for help) and 6 topics (Appx. Table cite93†8 ).\nL121: Source  | #Audio  | #Context  | Total\nL122: Meld  | 281  | ($\\times$3) 843  | 843\nL123: GigaSpeech  | 303  | ($\\times$2) 606  | 606\nL124: EmoV-DB  | ($\\times$4) 436  | 109  | 436\nL125: Total  | 1,020  | 1,558  | 1,885\nL126: Table 2: Statistics of AEQ-Bench. There are 1,885 English instances, each pairing an audio utterance with a textual context. ($\\times n$) means the number at the cell is $n$ times to the neighbouring number at the same row.\nL127: AEQ-Bench covers two complementary evaluation tasks, as illustrated in Fig. cite72†2 and cite73†3 :\nL128: #### 1) Same utterance, different contexts.\nL129: The first type of data focuses on the same utterance audio used in different contexts (A–Cs), where background framing shifts its interpretation. For example, the statement “Then just give him some money” could occur in a discussion about supporting a struggling relative, implying personal aid, or in a conversation about backing a political candidate, suggesting financial sponsorship. Despite the identical wording, the empathic strategies required are very different. (See the example in Fig. cite94†5 ).\nL130: #### 2) Same context, different utterances.\nL131: The second type of data involves the same context and utterance audio in different emotional tones (C-As). Although the literal words remain identical, the affective delivery changes the underlying meaning. For instance, an angry “Sometimes you really need to quarrel to solve problems” conveys frustration and a need for validation, whereas the same sentence spoken calmly suggests a pragmatic and constructive view of conflict.\nL132: Models should recognise the emotional signal and adjust their response style accordingly. (See the example in Fig. cite95†6 ).\nL133: ### 3.2 Benchmark Construction\nL134: \nL135: Our benchmark is constructed in three steps:\nL136: \nL137: #### 1) Audio Filtering.\nL138: \nL139: Because our benchmark focused on empathy evaluation, we filtered out scenarios of (i) Physical actions, (ii) Mathematical reasoning or complex task completion, and (iii) Targeting real people. More details are in Appx. cite37†F .\nL140: #### 2) Contexts Generation.\nL141: \nL142: We adopted GPT to frame different contexts for the retained data.\nL143: \nL144: $\\bullet$ A–Cs evaluate the ability to associate a specific context. Meld and GigaSpeech both have one specific audio for each utterance. Meld are daily short colloquial conversations that could fit in various contexts. GigaSpeech are more domain-specific and formal ones, restricting their flexibility. Therefore, we set 3 contexts for each Meld audio and 2 contexts for each GigaSpeech audio.\nL145: $\\bullet$ C–As evaluate the hearing ability of models. Different tones reflect the speaker’s different attitude towards the same context, which requires models to infer the speaker’s role or standpoint given a specific context. EmoV-DB recorded utterances with different explicit emotions. Here, we set only one context for all the different tones.\nL146: #### 3) Quality Validation.\nL147: To further validate the data quality of AEQ-Bench, we first prompted GPT to generate a plausible explanation for why the context and utterance are coherent and provide a reference response. Then, human annotators manually verified that each constructed context is coherent.^{3}^{3} 3 More annotation details are discussed in Appx. cite37†F . Specifically, they checked: (i) The context plausibly leads to the audio.\nL148: (ii) For A–Cs, the contexts are semantically different; For C-As, all the emotions are explainable in such contexts.\nL149: ### 3.3 Evaluation Metrics\nL150: \nL151: We evaluate from two aspects: linguistic and paralinguistic. modality reliance, naturalness, coherence, supportiveness, and discrimination are evaluated as linguistic features. For paralinguistic, we focus on delivery.^{4}^{4} 4 We also attempted to evaluate paralinguistic emotions, yet it cannot work. We discussed details in the Limitation section. Model judges are shown in Table cite96†3 and more details in Appx. cite40†G and cite50†H .\nL152: Judge  | Modality  | Metrics\nL153: --- | --- | ---\nL154: GPT-5  | Text  | M.R., Nat., Disc.\nL155: OLMs  | Audio  | Coh., Sup., Delivery\nL156: Table 3: The automatic judges for evaluation metrics.\nL157: \nL158: $\\bullet$ Modality Reliance (M.R.): Which modality does the model rely on to generate the response?\nL159: \nL160: $\\bullet$ Coherence (Coh.): Is the response logically consistent to the context and utterance? (semantic check for (Good, Fair, Poor))  cite97†Ickes et al. (2000) .\nL161: $\\bullet$ Naturalness (Nat.): How natural is the response? More human-like responses have higher scores with the range (1-4) cite98†Kühne et al. (2020) .\nL162: \nL163: $\\bullet$ Discrimination (Disc.): Across different contexts/tones, do the model responses vary? Higher scores are for those tailored for context/tone with the range (NA, 1–6) cite97†Ickes et al. (2000) .\nL164: $\\bullet$ Supportiveness (Sup.) : Imagine yourself in this situation. How supportive does the response feel? Here, we consider acknowledgment of feelings, perspective-taking, supportive intent (comfort/encouragement/help), and non-judgmental language for (Good, Fair, Poor) cite99†Rogers (1957) .\nL165: \nL166: $\\bullet$ Delivery: How supportive the response sounds? Here we consider the tone of voice, pacing, pauses, etc., for (Good, Fair, Poor) cite100†Burleson and Kunkel (2009) ; cite79†Loveys et al. (2021) .\nL167: ## 4 Experimental Settings\nL168: \nL169: #### Baselines.\nL170: \nL171: 1) Qwen3-Omni cite101†Xu et al. (2025b) , 2) Qwen2.5-Omni cite86†Xu et al. (2025a) , 3) Qwen2-Audio cite102†Chu et al. (2024) , 4) Qwen-audio cite103†Chu et al. (2023) , 5) SALMONN cite104†Tang et al. () , 6) LLaMA cite105†Fang et al. (2025) , 7) Flamingo cite106†Ghosh and Duraiswami () , 8) Baichuan cite107†Li et al. (2025) , and 9) GPT cite108†Hurst et al. (2024) . (See Appx. cite33†B ).\nL172: #### Human Annotation.\nL173: \nL174: For empathy judgement, we compared OLMs’ results to human’s. Audio responses were sampled evenly from each OLM. For each model pair, their responses to the same instance were evaluated by the same annotator to ensure consistent judgment; also, inter-annotator consistency was computed based on models’ ranking within each pair. Details are described in Appx. cite59†I .\nL175: #### Consistency Measurement.\nL176: Because empathetic scores are relatively subjective, for the Inter-annotator consistency, annotations are considered consistent if any of the following conditions hold: (i) the paired models receive different scores but the same rank order from both annotators; (ii) the paired models are tied in score by both annotators; or (iii) one annotator records a tie, and the other reports a score difference $\\leq$ 1.\nL177: For Human–OLM judge consistency, each model response is first assigned the average score of its two human annotators. The resulting average rank for each model pair is then compared against the OLM-judge ranking, following the aforementioned conditions.\nL178: Model  | T  | A  | T+A  | F  | Norm\nL179: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL180: GPT  | 0  | 18  | 82  | 0  | 0.91\nL181: LLaMA  | 1  | 36  | 62  | 1  | 0.81\nL182: Baichuan  | 6  | 34  | 59  | 1  | 0.79\nL183: Qwen3-Omni  | 1  | 24  | 74  | 0  | 0.87\nL184: Qwen2.5-Omni  | 0  | 25  | 74  | 0  | 0.87\nL185: Qwen2-Audio  | 4  | 18  | 52  | 25  | 0.63\nL186: Qwen-Audio  | 5  | 18  | 65  | 12  | 0.77\nL187: SALMONN  | 13  | 27  | 57  | 3  | 0.77\nL188: Flamingo  | 10  | 36  | 47  | 7  | 0.70\nL189: Table 4: Evaluation on input modality reliance. The values indicate the proportion (%) of responses based on text only (T), audio only (A), both text and audio (T+A), or failure to respond (F). Normalised score (Norm) is the sum of the value T+A and the average of T and A.\nL190: ## 5 Results on Omni-Models as Responders\nL191: Audio  | Model  | Meld  | GigaSpeech  | EmoV-DB  | Overall  | Normalised Scores\nL192: Output  | ..Nat.  | Disc.  | ..Nat...  | Disc.  | Nat.  | Disc.  | Nat.  | Disc.  | Nat. (Rank)  | Disc. (Rank)\nL193: ✓  | GPT  | 3.95  | 4.85  | 3.93  | 4.38  | 3.97  | 2.17  | 3.95  | 4.22  | 0.98 (1)  | 0.64 (6)\nL194: ✓  | LLaMA  | 3.88  | 4.81  | 3.77  | 4.79  | 3.88  | 2.70  | 3.85  | 4.47  | 0.95 (4)  | 0.69 (4)\nL195: ✓  | Baichuan  | 3.90  | 5.15  | 3.81  | 4.95  | 3.94  | 3.08  | 3.88  | 4.74  | 0.96 (2)  | 0.75 (2)\nL196: ✓  | Qwen3-Omni  | 3.61  | 5.14  | 3.88  | 5.02  | 3.93  | 2.77  | 3.77  | 4.71  | 0.92 (5)  | 0.74 (3)\nL197: ✓  | Qwen2.5-Omni  | 3.87  | 4.38  | 3.87  | 4.63  | 3.94  | 2.34  | 3.89  | 4.17  | 0.96 (2)  | 0.63 (7)\nL198: -  | Qwen2-Audio  | 3.01  | 4.63  | 2.32  | 3.85  | 3.18  | 2.93  | 2.82  | 4.00  | 0.61 (7)  | 0.60 (8)\nL199: -  | Qwen-Audio  | 2.25  | 4.78  | 2.46  | 5.01  | 2.48  | 4.63  | 2.37  | 4.86  | 0.46 (8)  | 0.77 (1)\nL200: -  | SALMONN  | 3.25  | 4.61  | 3.18  | 4.66  | 3.31  | 3.09  | 3.24  | 4.39  | 0.75 (6)  | 0.68 (5)\nL201: -  | Flamingo  | 2.40  | 4.10  | 2.31  | 3.79  | 1.95  | 2.66  | 2.27  | 3.73  | 0.42 (9)  | 0.55 (9)\nL202: Table 5: Naturalness (Nat.) measures how human-like the responses are (ranges 1-4 and the higher the more human-like). Discrimination (Disc.) assesses the model’s ability to distinguish responses across varying contexts (Meld & GigaSpeech) or audio tone (EmoV-DB) (ranges 1-6, and the higher the better). They both are measured on text and evaluated by GPT-5-mini (with manual checking) following the text evaluation practice cite109†Fu et al. (2024) .\nL203: We first discuss model responses with empathy. Table cite110†4 shows Modality Reliance of baselines, Table cite111†5 their Naturalness and Discrimination, and Table cite112†7 Delivery evaluated by human and OLM-judges. We observe that: (1) OLMs with audio outputs outperform text-only-output models. GPT and Qwen’s Omni series represent the top-tier audio synthesis quality. These indicate the benefits of multimodal integration in learning empathy.\nL204: (2) Models’ performance varies on Discrimination and Naturalness, indicating they may be decoupled abilities.\nL205: $\\bullet$ Modality Reliance. As shown in Table cite110†4 , all OLMs primarily rely on both audio and text modalities (over 50%). GPT demonstrates the best multimodal integration (normalised to 0.91), followed by Qwen’s omni series (0.87). Earlier audio analysis models exhibit higher failure rates and weaker multimodal grounding, i.e., Qwen’s audio series.\nL206: $\\bullet$ Naturalness. Among OLMs, GPT achieves the highest overall Naturalness (normalised to 0.98), closely followed by Baichuan and Qwen2.5-Omni (both 0.96). It suggests that recent OLMs can produce responses better resembling human conversational style. In contrast, Flamingo and the Qwen-Audio series exhibit substantially lower Naturalness, often providing analytical or descriptive content rather than natural, conversational replies.\nL207: $\\bullet$ Discrimination. In contrast, Qwen-Audio attains the highest Discrimination score (normalised to 0.77), particularly on the EmoV-DB subset. It indicates strong sensitivity to varying emotional tones of input audio. However, this sensitivity is primarily due to analytical variation (not communicating), as evidenced by its poor Naturalness.\nL208: $\\bullet$ Delivery. Only OLMs with audio outputs are evaluated for the paralinguistic metric. Both Human and OLM-judges consistently rank GPT, Qwen3-Omni, and Qwen2.5-Omni as the top-tier models in paralinguistic quality, indicating superior control over prosody and vocal delivery compared to LLaMa and Baichuan.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCtD: Composition through Decompositionin Emergent Communication (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10169v1)\nciteturn30867view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10169v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 750\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdjusted Similarity Measures and a Violation of Expectations (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10641v1)\nciteturn30867view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10641v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 379\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDanQing: An Up-to-Date Large-Scale Chinese Vision-Language Pre-training Dataset (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10305v1)\nciteturn30867view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10305v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 402\n"
